sponsored result, or the undocumented-workers story escalates in the final weeks, the race could tighten toward tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Jim Pillen (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's governor's race pits incumbent Republican Jim Pillen against Democrat Lynne Walz, with two independents also on the ballot. Pillen holds structural advantages in a state whose partisan lean favors Republicans by about ten points, and most race raters call the contest Solid Republican, with a couple rating it Likely Republican. Prediction markets give Walz roughly a one-in-ten chance of an upset.\n\nRecent polling is mixed. Nonpartisan September surveys showed Pillen ahead by eight to seventeen points, while Democratic-sponsored polls found the race near tied, including a 40-40 result that drew wide coverage. A New York Times report that Pillen employed undocumented workers while backing immigration enforcement, plus the Nebraska Farm Bureau's neutrality after backing him in 2022, have given Walz talking points. The combined evidence points to a Pillen lead of roughly eight points.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0219, "margin": -11.34}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0357, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0267, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0322, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0312, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0585, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0639, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0714, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0694, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0605, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0982, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.0987, "margin": -8.0}]}